<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yamaguchi (city)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yamaguchi is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honshu">Honshu</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-prefecture">Yamaguchi Prefecture</a>. In the 15th and 16th centuries it was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouchi-clan">Ōuchi clan</a>, who laid it out on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and made it a centre of culture where the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesshu-toyo">Sesshū</a> worked and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a> preached in 1551. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/choshu-domain">Chōshū Domain</a> moved its seat there in 1863.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yamaguchi-city.jpg" alt="Yamaguchi (city)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wiki708 at Japanese Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yamaguchi_Xavier_Memorial_Church.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
